# Luma crash evidence: journal catalog (journalctl -x shows this text).

-- e6f1229b2154445eaaf8f207d149fb1f
Subject: The computer restarted unexpectedly; the processor's Crash Log says why
Defined-By: Luma
Support: https://projectluma.org/vitals
Documentation: file:///usr/share/doc/luma-vitals/026-luma-vitals.md

Before this boot the machine was reset by its own firmware or platform
controller, not shut down: typically a processor core or controller stopped
responding and the platform restarted itself. Nothing reaches the journal when
that happens. The firmware kept an Intel Crash Log in the ACPI Boot Error
Record Table, and Intel's iclg decoded it at this boot.

The LUMA_CRASHLOG_TRIAGE fields are iclg's cause tags, for example
CORE_TIMEOUT.MULTIPLE_STUCK_TRANSACTIONS (a core stopped completing work) or
RESET_CAUSE.GLOBAL_RESET.PMC_FW (the power management controller reset the
platform). LUMA_CRASHLOG_IP names the processor's last instruction pointer as
a kernel module and offset when the previous boot's module map was kept.

The raw record (BERT, BERT.data), iclg's decoded JSON (crashlog-decoded.json),
the triage (crashlog-triage.txt) and a summary (crashlog.json) are in
LUMA_CRASH_DIRECTORY. Include them when reporting the problem. They describe
processor and platform register state (addresses and status bits), not
files or documents.

The same record is decoded only once, however many boots the firmware keeps
reporting it.
